type: statement
kind: remark
---

One might be tempted to instead map `u` from `Rng::next_u64` via the operation $2^{-64}u$ and compare to $p$, but this implementation would be wrong, because of the fashion in which `f64` instances are allocated in the interval $[0, 1]$.
There are $1023 \times 2^{52}$ instances of `f64` in $[0, 1]$, because there are $52$ bits of instances in each of the diadic intervals $[2^{-1023}, 2^{-1022}), \ldots, [2^{-1}, 2^0)$, there is $1$, and there is $-0$.
These instances are not equi-distant (since the diadic intervals are increasing in size), and so there is a bias accumulation of points near $0$.
